1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,054
JAMIE: JPMorgan's up four point eighty-nine percent on the back of Morgan Stanley posting record revenue, which is great—until you remember Buffett just called the market a casino, and now I'm wondering if we're all just playing blackjack with our 401ks.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/morgan-stanley-ms-earnings-q2-2026-.html

2
00:00:13,054 --> 00:00:30,884
ALEX: So yesterday's C P I surprise has traders convinced the Fed's done tightening, and they're already pricing in rate cuts by the fall. Morgan Stanley's trading revenue jumped sixty-nine percent because the market's front-running a policy pivot that hasn't been announced yet. The Fed's still hawkish in public, but Wall Street's already moved the furniture.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/morgan-stanley-ms-earnings-q2-2026-.html

3
00:00:30,884 --> 00:01:02,404
ALEX: The inflation surprise is doing exactly what it's supposed to do—it's turned the market into a one-directional bet on rate cuts. S&P up zero point seven-five percent to seven thousand five hundred seventy-one. Nasdaq's the real story: up one point four-six percent to twenty-six thousand two hundred forty-nine. JPMorgan's up four point eight-nine percent, Nvidia four point two-one percent. But here's the thing nobody wants to talk about: the Russell Two Thousand dropped zero point eight-three percent. We're not seeing breadth. We're seeing a narrow rally in mega-cap financials and chips, and everyone else is getting left behind.

4
00:01:02,404 --> 00:01:11,638
JAMIE: So it's the inflation cool-down that's supposed to save us, except the people who actually benefit from that cool-down are the ones who already have the most money.

5
00:01:11,638 --> 00:01:34,244
MAYA: And Lily's down three point four-five percent, PepsiCo down two point six-one—the companies that live and die by whether regular people can afford to buy stuff are tanking because, I guess, we all just decided to stop eating and drinking. The V I X is down five point seventy-seven percent though, so everyone's convinced the scary part is over, which is hilarious because that's usually when the scary part starts.

6
00:01:34,244 --> 00:01:51,437
ALEX: Broadcom up three point four-six percent, A M D up three point five percent. These are the companies that benefit from a weaker dollar and a Fed pivot in the same breath. Cisco's down three point six-eight percent because nobody's buying networking gear when they're too busy watching the Fed move the furniture.

7
00:01:51,437 --> 00:02:00,670
JAMIE: So we've gone from Buffett saying the market's a casino to the market proving him right by betting the house on a policy announcement that hasn't happened yet.

8
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:12,132
ALEX: Morgan Stanley's equities trading revenue surged and that's the entire earnings beat right there. The bank posted strong net income, but strip out that one trading desk and you're looking at a pretty ordinary quarter.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/morgan-stanley-ms-earnings-q2-2026-.html

9
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:18,182
JAMIE: So the bank's profit is basically a single department's volatility play. What happens when the market stops moving?

10
00:02:18,182 --> 00:02:41,424
MAYA: Right now? Nothing, because the market I S moving—it's been moving nonstop since the inflation number dropped. Everyone's convinced the Fed's about to pivot, so traders are getting paid to sit at their desks and guess which way the next announcement goes. Morgan Stanley's equities traders made more money in this quarter than they did in the entire first half of last year. That's not skill, that's just riding a one-way bet.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/morgan-stanley-ms-earnings-q2-2026-.html

11
00:02:41,424 --> 00:03:00,210
ALEX: And here's what the market's not pricing in: Morgan Stanley's fixed income trading revenue actually fell. Bonds are boring when everyone thinks rates are about to drop—nobody needs to hedge, nobody's scared, the vol just evaporates. So you've got this lopsided picture where equities are screaming and credit's silent, which is exactly the setup where someone gets blindsided.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/morgan-stanley-ms-earnings-q2-2026-.html

12
00:03:00,210 --> 00:03:05,941
JAMIE: So the market's not celebrating Morgan Stanley's earnings, it's celebrating that the fear trade finally switched off.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/morgan-stanley-ms-earnings-q2-2026-.html

13
00:03:05,941 --> 00:03:22,497
MAYA: Which means next quarter, when the Fed doesn't cut and traders realize they've been front-running a speech, Morgan Stanley's equities desk is going to post numbers that look like a typo. The bank's stock will crater, and everyone will act shocked that a quarter built entirely on one-directional betting didn't hold up.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/morgan-stanley-ms-earnings-q2-2026-.html

14
00:03:22,497 --> 00:03:35,869
JAMIE: Warren Buffett on the market today: 'It's tough to find values when everybody is preferring gambling.' The Oracle is basically saying retail is doing the Fed's job for them—they're so busy speculating that the actual price discovery mechanism is just broken.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/warren-buffett-on-the-market-today-its-tough-to-find-values-when-everybody-is-preferring-gambling.html

15
00:03:35,869 --> 00:03:50,516
ALEX: When Buffett says he can't find value, he means the marginal dollar in the market is not interested in cash flows or balance sheets. It's interested in whether the Fed moves the furniture. That's not a market correction—that's a market that's stopped being a market.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/buffett-says-trumps-pick-of-kevin-warsh-for-fed-chair-was-good-choice.html

16
00:03:50,516 --> 00:04:03,251
MAYA: A SpaceX vet just raised sixty-five million dollars to modernize wire harnesses—the literal cables that hold rockets together. Nobody's funding this because it's sexy, they're funding it because every aerospace company is still using wiring diagrams from the eighties.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/15/a-spacex-vet-raised-65m-to-pull-wire-harnesses-out-of-the-cold-war-era/

17
00:04:03,251 --> 00:04:16,305
JAMIE: So there's a company that builds AI models worth billions, and there's a company that bundles wires for satellites, and the wire company just got sixty-five million because the infrastructure is so broken that fixing it is the actual venture.

18
00:04:16,305 --> 00:04:24,265
MAYA: Anthropic and Blackstone are betting the next trillion-dollar AI business isn't building the model—it's embedding engineers inside enterprises to actually make the thing work.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/15/anthropic-blackstone-bet-the-next-trillion-dollar-ai-business-is-implementation-not-models/

19
00:04:24,265 --> 00:04:39,548
ALEX: So the narrative's shifting from 'we built a smarter G P T' to 'we hired someone to sit in your finance department and make sure the smarter G P T doesn't hallucinate your tax return.' That's not a product, that's a consulting firm with a neural network.

20
00:04:39,548 --> 00:04:49,736
JAMIE: PayPal's stock is soaring on rumors that Stripe and Advent Capital are circling for a buyout. A company that used to own the entire future of payments is now a takeover target.
  ↳ source: https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/article/paypal-stock-soars-on-report-of-buyout-proposal-from-stripe-advent-131101969.html

21
00:04:49,736 --> 00:05:01,835
MAYA: PayPal spent the last five years trying to convince everyone they were still relevant, and the market's answer is: sure, but only if someone else buys you. That's not a vote of confidence, that's a mercy acquisition.

22
00:05:01,835 --> 00:05:22,531
MAYA: The UK government just proposed a midnight social media curfew for sixteen and seventeen-year-olds—which sounds like a real policy until you read the fine print and realize teenagers can just opt out. So it's a curfew where the person it's supposed to restrict gets to vote on whether the curfew exists. That's like a speed limit that only applies if you agree with it.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c982857nlrlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

23
00:05:22,531 --> 00:05:37,814
JAMIE: Wait, so the government's theory is that a sixteen-year-old at midnight is going to see the notification and go, 'Oh, well, the Prime Minister has spoken, I suppose I'll put the phone down'? And if that doesn't work, they just click a button that says 'never mind'?

24
00:05:37,814 --> 00:05:57,236
ALEX: It's regulatory theater with an exit door. The government gets to announce they're protecting youth mental health, campaigners get to say it's a missed opportunity, and teenagers get to opt out, which means nobody's actually accountable when the mental health numbers don't improve. Everyone wins except the sixteen-year-olds who are supposed to benefit from a rule they're allowed to ignore.

25
00:05:57,236 --> 00:06:04,559
ALEX: Buffett can't find value because Morgan Stanley traders just turned the entire market into a one-way bet on something nobody's announced yet.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/15/morgan-stanley-ms-earnings-q2-2026-.html

26
00:06:04,559 --> 00:06:10,608
MAYA: The oracle meets the oracle pit—one's looking for cash flows, the other's just looking for the next move.

27
00:06:10,608 --> 00:06:15,066
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
